<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Datasets</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Experiments are carried out on several widely used, representative text-attributed graph datasets (Table <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Appendix A Datasets ‣ Unveiling the Vulnerability of Graph-LLMs: An Interpretable Multi-Dimensional Adversarial Attack on TAGs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).
In these datasets, each node is associated with a short textual description (e.g., paper title or abstract, product description), and edges encode citations.
Node features are obtained by encoding node texts with our encoding model.
We adopt stratified splits of 10%/10%/80% (train/val/test) for Cora, Citeseer, and PubMed, and 20%/20%/60% for ogbn-arxiv.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T5" class="ltx_table">
